   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Carlson and Sons Medical Group\nPatient: Eric Boyer DOB: 05/06/1970 Sex: M MRN: 900701\n\nEncounters:\n 2025-07-28 Alcohol use disorder (treated) BP 131/66 Wt 141\n 2024-07-01 Alcohol use disorder (treated) BP 127/68 Wt 129\n 2023-06-04 flu shot BP 127/65 Wt 128\n 2022-05-08 rash BP 137/69 Wt 128\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • Alcohol use disorder (treated) (abstinent 3.1 yrs; treatment IOP; relapses - controlled; meds: naltrexone 380 mg inj\n\nPatient History:\nEric Boyer, a 55-year-old male, has a significant history of alcohol use disorder, which he has been managing under our care since December 2015. His treatment has included intensive outpatient program (IOP) therapy and the use of naltrexone, a medication that has helped him maintain abstinence for over three years. His most recent relapse occurred approximately one year ago, and he has since resumed his treatment regimen without further incidents.\n\nRecent Visits:\nDuring the most recent visit on July 28, 2025, Mr. Boyer presented for his regularly scheduled follow-up regarding his alcohol use disorder. He reported no alcohol use since his last visit and continues to attend IOP sessions. His blood pressure was 131/66 mmHg, and his weight was 141 pounds.\n\nOn July 1, 2024, Mr. Boyer also followed up for his alcohol use disorder. His blood pressure was 127/68 mmHg, and his weight was 129 pounds. He reported adherence to his medication regimen with naltrexone and no signs of relapse.\n\nOn June 4, 2023, Mr. Boyer visited for a flu shot. His blood pressure at that time was 127/65 mmHg, and his weight was 128 pounds. He reported no issues related to his alcohol use disorder and no adverse effects from naltrexone.\n\nOn May 8, 2022, Mr. Boyer presented with a rash. His blood pressure was 137/69 mmHg, and his weight was 128 pounds. The rash was not related to his alcohol use disorder or naltrexone treatment.\n\nPhysical Examination:\nThroughout his visits, Mr. Boyer's physical examinations have been within normal limits. He has had no significant findings related to his alcohol use disorder or medication. His vital signs, including blood pressure and weight, have remained stable over the past few years.\n\nMedications:\nMr. Boyer is currently taking naltrexone 380 mg injections, which he has been on since his treatment began. There have been no reported side effects, and his adherence to this medication has been consistent.\n\nSocial History:\nMr. Boyer disclosed that he currently uses cigars, smoking about seven times a year. He started this habit on January 29, 2023, and has not"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "attempted to quit. He admits to minimizing the disclosure of his cigar use, likely due to awareness of its potential health implications.\n\nFamily History:\nMr. Boyer’s mother has a history of Alzheimer's disease, diagnosed at age 65. She is currently alive and well.\n\nCurrent Status:\nMr. Boyer's alcohol use disorder is well-controlled. He has abstinent for over years, with only one relapse in the past year. He continues to receive support through IOP and remains committed to his treatment plan. His current medication, naltrexone, has been effective in helping him maintain sobriety. Additionally, his cigar use is infrequent but should be monitored as part of his overall health management.\n\nConclusion:\nMr. Boyer’s ongoing commitment to his treatment and lifestyle adjustments has resulted in stable condition. controlled alcohol use disorder and adherence to naltrexone treatment indicate a positive prognosis. Regular follow-ups and continued support through IOP are recommended to maintain his sobriety and overall health.\n\nDr. Rebekah Hernandez\nSpecialty: Addiction Medicine\nPractice: Carlson and Sons Medical Group\n\n---\n\nThis clinical summary provides a comprehensive overview of Mr. Boyer’s medical history, recent visits, and current status. It highlights his controlled alcohol use disorder and consistent medication adherence, supported by stable vital signs and normal physical examination findings.\n"
